## Managing Student Records

A gradebook is a perfect Map use case: student names are keys, grades are values. Let's build the full set of operations.

### Setting Up the Gradebook

```xiom
let gradebook: Map[Str,Int] = Map[Str,Int].new();
```

### Adding and Looking Up Grades

```xiom
gradebook.insert("Alice", 95);
gradebook.insert("Bob", 87);
gradebook.insert("Charlie", 92);

fn get_grade(gb: Map[Str,Int], name: Str) -> Str {
  match gb.get(name) {
    Some(grade) -> return string.int_to_str(grade);
    None -> return "No grade recorded";
  }
}
```

### Updating a Grade (Regrade)

```xiom
fn update_grade(gb: Map[Str,Int], name: Str, new_grade: Int) -> Bool {
  if gb.contains(name) {
    gb.insert(name, new_grade);
    return true;
  }
  else {
    return false;
  }
}
```

### Calculating the Class Average

Since Map doesn't iterate directly like Vec, we collect keys into a Vec first:

```xiom
fn class_average(gb: Map[Str,Int], names: Vec[Str]) -> Int {
  let total: Int = 0;
  let count: Int = 0;
  for name in names {
    match gb.get(name) {
      Some(grade) -> { total = total + grade; count = count + 1; }
      None -> {}
    }
  }
  if count == 0 { return 0; }
  return total / count;
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let gb: Map[Str,Int] = Map[Str,Int].new();
  gb.insert("Alice", 95);
  gb.insert("Bob", 87);
  gb.insert("Charlie", 92);

  let students: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  students.push("Alice");
  students.push("Bob");
  students.push("Charlie");

  match gb.get("Alice") {
    Some(g) -> io.println("Alice: " + string.int_to_str(g));
    None -> io.println("Alice: not found");
  }

  let avg = class_average(gb, students);
  io.println("Average: " + string.int_to_str(avg));
  return 0;
}
```

